Divide first. For $y=\dfrac{x^{2}+1}{x-1}$,

$$
y=x+1+\frac{2}{x-1}
\tag{3}
$$

so $y=x+1$ is the oblique asymptote and $x=1$ the vertical one. The leftover term $\dfrac{2}{x-1}$ is positive for $x>1$ (curve above the line) and negative for $x<1$ (curve below it). Then $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=1-\dfrac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}=0$ gives $(x-1)^{2}=2$, so $x=1\pm\sqrt2$, with $y=2\pm2\sqrt2$. Since $\dfrac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}=\dfrac{4}{(x-1)^{3}}$, the point $(1+\sqrt2,\,2+2\sqrt2)$ is a local minimum and $(1-\sqrt2,\,2-2\sqrt2)$ is a local maximum. The local maximum ($y\approx-0.83$) is **lower** than the local minimum ($y\approx4.83$): this is normal when a vertical asymptote separates the branches, and no point of the curve has $y$ between these two values. The curve meets the $y$-axis at $(0,-1)$ and never meets the $x$-axis because $x^{2}+1>0$.
